@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.804 = private unnamed_addr constant [1800 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for modules that use the same names as Python standard-library\0Amodules.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AReusing a standard-library module name for the name of a module increases\0Athe difficulty of reading and maintaining the code, and can cause\0Anon-obvious errors. Readers may mistake the first-party module for the\0Astandard-library module and vice versa.\0A\0AStandard-library modules can be marked as exceptions to this rule via the\0A[`lint.flake8-builtins.allowed-modules`] configuration option.\0A\0ABy default, the module path relative to the project root or [`src`] directories is considered,\0Aso a top-level `logging.py` or `logging/__init__.py` will clash with the builtin `logging`\0Amodule, but `utils/logging.py`, for example, will not. With the\0A[`lint.flake8-builtins.strict-checking`] option set to `true`, only the last component\0Aof the module name is considered, so `logging.py`, `utils/logging.py`, and\0A`utils/logging/__init__.py` will all trigger the rule.\0A\0AThis rule is not applied to stub files, as the name of a stub module is out\0Aof the control of the author of the stub file. Instead, a stub should aim to\0Afaithfully emulate the runtime module it is stubbing.\0A\0AAs of Python 3.13, errors from modules that use the same name as\0Astandard-library modules now display a custom message.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```console\0A$ touch random.py\0A$ python3 -c 'from random import choice'\0ATraceback (most recent call last):\0A  File \22<string>\22, line 1, in <module>\0A    from random import choice\0AImportError: cannot import name 'choice' from 'random' (consider renaming '/random.py' since it has the same name as the standard library module named 'random' and prevents importing that standard library module)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.flake8-builtins.allowed-modules`\0A- `lint.flake8-builtins.strict-checking`\0A", align 1
