@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.938 = private unnamed_addr constant [1056 x i8] c"## What it does\0ADetects attempts to use `super` without parentheses.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe [`super()` callable](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#super)\0Acan be used inside method definitions to create a proxy object that\0Adelegates attribute access to a superclass of the current class. Attempting\0Ato access attributes on `super` itself, however, instead of the object\0Areturned by a call to `super()`, will raise `AttributeError`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Animal:\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def speak():\0A        return \22This animal says something.\22\0A\0A\0Aclass Dog(Animal):\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def speak():\0A        original_speak = super.speak()  # ERROR: `super.speak()`\0A        return f\22{original_speak} But as a dog, it barks!\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Animal:\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def speak():\0A        return \22This animal says something.\22\0A\0A\0Aclass Dog(Animal):\0A    @staticmethod\0A    def speak():\0A        original_speak = super().speak()  # Correct: `super().speak()`\0A        return f\22{original_speak} But as a dog, it barks!\22\0A```\0A", align 1
